{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786480251,"nonce":"rfGvdOBWeVpby4NcvBRFUp+y+S0=","title":"**What is wrong.** The contract caps `?type=` on the group timeline at **34 items** while its own `AlertEventType` enum has **36**. `maxItems` was never updated when `alert.snoozed` and `alert.unsnoozed` joined the enum.","message":"**What that costs.** A caller asking for EVERY type it is allowed to see — \"show me everything on this group\" — writes a request **its own generated client refuses to send**, while the server would have answered it happily. The failure is entirely client-side and entirely invisible to the server, so it will not appear in any log.\n\n**Why it matters.** A filter ceiling below the enum it filters is a bound that can only ever be wrong. It cannot protect anything (36 items is not a load concern) and it can only reject legitimate requests. It also drifts again the moment a 37th event type is added, because nothing ties the two numbers together.\n\n**Where.** `api/openapi/openapi.yaml` — the `type` query parameter on `getAlertGroupTimeline` (`maxItems: 34`) and the `AlertEventType` enum.\n\n**Pinned by** the skipped `TestBUG_TheTimelineTypeFilterCeilingIsSmallerThanTheEnum` in `internal/grouping/api/groups_contract_test.go:954`.\n\n**Done when.** The ceiling is at least the size of the enum, the generated client and validators are regenerated (G3/G4), and the pin is unskipped. Worth considering whether the bound should exist at all — and if it stays, whether anything can make it fail loudly the next time the enum grows, since that is how it broke this time.","files":null}]}